WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Many string defects come from confusing concepts that sound similar.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.920
Size is the current number of elements in a string.

3
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:13.420
Capacity is storage that may be available without reallocation.

4
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.960
A buffer length is the amount of memory available to write into.

5
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:20.100
A string length is the amount of meaningful data.

6
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.960
Those values may be related, but they are not interchangeable.

7
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.440
Byte count and character count can also differ.

8
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.620
In many encodings, one visible character may require multiple bytes.

9
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:39.560
A limit expressed in bytes may be appropriate for storage or protocol size, while a limit expressed in characters may be appropriate for display or policy.

10
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.140
Code should name which limit it is enforcing.

11
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.660
Null termination needs the same care.

12
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:52.040
A C API may require a null-terminated string, while a length-aware API may accept data with embedded null characters.

13
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:59.500
A null terminator can be missing, appear earlier than expected, or be irrelevant for binary data.

14
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.680
Defensive code does not assume that a terminator proves a buffer is safe or complete.

15
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.920
Bounds should be checked before indexing, copying, formatting, or converting.

16
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:14.940
Truncation should not happen silently unless it is an explicit and documented behavior.

17
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:25.040
If truncation is allowed, the code should make clear whether it preserves valid encoding, whether it changes security meaning, and whether the operator needs to know it occurred.

18
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:31.240
The practical rule is to carry length information alongside data and validate it before use.

19
00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:37.500
Avoid APIs that require guessing how much data is present when a safer, length-aware option is available.
